WEBVTT

00:00:07.943 --> 00:00:11.804
Sherlock Holmes and The Red Circle.

00:00:11.864 --> 00:00:16.915
By Sir Arthur Conan Doyle.

00:00:16.976 --> 00:00:29.839
Copyright Blackcat Publishing.

00:00:29.899 --> 00:00:35.554
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle.

00:00:35.614 --> 00:00:43.020
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle was born in Edinburgh, Scotland, on 22 May 1859.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.672
When he was a young boy, he liked reading books.

00:00:47.732 --> 00:00:52.783
In 1876, he began studying medicine at Edinburgh University.

00:00:52.843 --> 00:00:56.734
One of his professors was Dr. Joseph Bell.

00:00:56.794 --> 00:01:02.446
Dr. Bell watched his patients closely so that he could understand their illness.

00:01:02.506 --> 00:01:09.038
Later, Dr. Bell became Conan Doyle's model for Sherlock Holmes.

00:01:09.098 --> 00:01:12.269
In 1882, Conan Doyle became a doctor.

00:01:12.329 --> 00:01:21.560
He did not have many patients, so he began writing his first Sherlock Holmes story, A Study in Scarlet, 1887.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:25.421
The story was a big success.

00:01:25.481 --> 00:01:31.360
In 1891, he wrote Sherlock Holmes stories for a monthly magazine called The Strand.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:38.080
Soon, the clever detective and his friend Dr. Watson became very popular in Great Britain.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:43.751
When Conan Doyle stopped writing Sherlock Holmes stories, his readers got angry.

00:01:43.811 --> 00:01:51.494
Finally, in 1901, he started writing another Sherlock Holmes adventure called The Hound of the Baskervilles.

00:01:51.554 --> 00:01:53.724
A lot of other stories followed.

00:01:53.784 --> 00:02:00.320
His readers were very pleased, and he continued writing about the famous detective until 1927.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:20.594
Some famous Sherlock Holmes books are the sign of four, 1890, The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes, 1892, The Return of Sherlock Holmes, 1905, The Valley of Fear, 1915, and The Case Book of Sherlock Holmes, 1927.

00:02:20.654 --> 00:02:26.356
The Red Circle is part of the book, His Last Bow, 1917.

00:02:26.416 --> 00:02:33.600
Conan Doyle died in England on the 7th of July 1930.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:45.156
Sherlock Holmes and Dr. Watson The story you are going to read takes place in London at the start of the 20th century.

00:02:45.216 --> 00:02:52.710
It is about London's famous fictional detective Sherlock Holmes and his good friend, Dr. Watson.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:57.893
Dr. Watson helps Holmes with his detective cases.

00:02:57.953 --> 00:03:07.056
When Sherlock Holmes moved to London in 1874, he did not have much money, and his family had to help him.

00:03:07.116 --> 00:03:15.049
In 1881, he decided to share the cost of his rooms at 221B Baker Street with Dr. John H.

00:03:15.108 --> 00:03:20.390
Watson, and this is how Holmes and Watson became good friends.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.948
Watson tells us a lot of things about Holmes.

00:03:24.008 --> 00:03:29.567
He is not a difficult person to live with because he is quiet and has regular habits.

00:03:29.627 --> 00:03:36.649
For example, he usually goes to bed around 10 at night and gets up very early in the morning.

00:03:36.709 --> 00:03:45.662
He works on his cases with a lot of energy, but sometimes he gets very bored and sits in the living room and does nothing.

00:03:45.722 --> 00:03:52.342
Watson also tells us that he is untidy and his desk is always full of papers.

00:03:52.402 --> 00:03:56.782
Mrs. Hudson is the housekeeper who tidies the rooms.

00:03:56.842 --> 00:04:02.494
We know that Holmes plays the violin and wears Persian slippers at home.

00:04:02.554 --> 00:04:07.605
He often uses chemistry equipment to help him solve cases.

00:04:07.665 --> 00:04:17.522
Holmes is an excellent detective and he often surprises Watson with his solutions to difficult cases.

00:04:17.582 --> 00:04:21.139
Chapter 1.

00:04:21.198 --> 00:04:24.220
Mrs. Warren's problem.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.101
Well, Mrs. Warren, I'm sorry, but I can't help you.

00:04:29.161 --> 00:04:31.132
I have other things to do.

00:04:31.192 --> 00:04:35.254
Said Sherlock Holmes, looking at the papers on his desk.

00:04:35.314 --> 00:04:38.354
But Mrs. Warren did not go away.

00:04:38.414 --> 00:04:41.566
You helped my lodger, Mr. Hobbs, last year?

00:04:41.626 --> 00:04:44.597
Said Mrs. Warren, looking at Holmes.

00:04:44.657 --> 00:04:46.528
Yes, I remember.

00:04:46.588 --> 00:04:47.820
Said Holmes.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.634
Mr. Hope said you are a kind, helpful man.

00:04:51.694 --> 00:04:53.624
Why can't you help me?"

00:04:53.684 --> 00:04:55.425
asked Mrs. Warren.

00:04:55.485 --> 00:05:02.136
Holmes looked at her and said, all right, Mrs. Warren, tell me about your problem.

00:05:02.196 --> 00:05:06.847
I understand your new lodger always stays in his rooms and you never see him.

00:05:06.907 --> 00:05:09.608
There's nothing strange about that.

00:05:09.668 --> 00:05:13.079
But I'm really afraid and I can't sleep.

00:05:13.139 --> 00:05:14.677
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:05:14.737 --> 00:05:19.927
I hear his quick footsteps from morning to night, but I never see him.

00:05:19.987 --> 00:05:21.978
Why is he hiding?

00:05:22.038 --> 00:05:26.600
My husband is worried too, but he is away all day at work.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.612
I'm alone at home with the girl who helps me clean, and I'm very frightened."

00:05:31.672 --> 00:05:37.580
Holmes looked at her kindly and said, please sit down Mrs. Warren.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:42.196
Now, if I decide to help you, I must know everything.

00:05:42.256 --> 00:05:43.656
Do you understand?

00:05:43.716 --> 00:05:47.448
The smallest thing can be the most important.

00:05:47.508 --> 00:05:52.480
You said that the man came ten days ago and paid you for two weeks.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:53.780
Is that correct?

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:54.660
Yes.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.261
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:05:56.321 --> 00:06:02.148
I asked him to pay two pounds a week for a small sitting room and a bedroom at the top of the house.

00:06:02.208 --> 00:06:04.694
Well, asked Holmes.

00:06:04.754 --> 00:06:11.080
He said, I'll pay you five pounds a week if I can have the rooms on my conditions.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.121
I'm a poor woman, sir.

00:06:13.181 --> 00:06:15.623
Mr. Warren doesn't make much money.

00:06:15.683 --> 00:06:17.964
I needed the extra money.

00:06:18.024 --> 00:06:27.062
The man gave me ten pounds and said, I can give you ten pounds every two weeks for a long time on my conditions.

00:06:27.121 --> 00:06:28.973
What were his conditions?

00:06:29.033 --> 00:06:30.316
Asked Holmes.

00:06:30.376 --> 00:06:34.571
Well, he wanted a key to the house, and that was alright.

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.692
Lodges off want a key.

00:06:36.752 --> 00:06:39.845
And he did not want anyone to disturb him.

00:06:39.905 --> 00:06:44.770
There is nothing strange about that, Mrs. Warren, said Holmes.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:48.152
No, but I never see him.

00:06:48.212 --> 00:06:50.124
No one sees him.

00:06:50.184 --> 00:06:53.978
He only went out of the house on the first night.

00:06:54.037 --> 00:06:56.809
Oh, he went out on the first night.

00:06:56.869 --> 00:06:57.999
Asked Holmes.

00:06:58.059 --> 00:06:59.099
Yes, sir.

00:06:59.159 --> 00:07:02.810
And he came back very late when we were all in bed.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:04.550
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:06.481
But his meals?

00:07:06.541 --> 00:07:07.771
Asked Holmes.

00:07:07.831 --> 00:07:12.732
Oh, we leave his meals on a chair outside his room when he rings the bell.

00:07:12.792 --> 00:07:14.152
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:07:14.212 --> 00:07:17.376
And he rings again when he finishes eating.

00:07:17.436 --> 00:07:24.012
If he wants anything, he writes it in capital letters on a piece of paper and leaves it on the chair.

00:07:24.072 --> 00:07:26.403
He writes in capital letters.

00:07:26.463 --> 00:07:27.653
Asked Holmes.

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:31.985
Yes sir, just the word in pencil and nothing more.

00:07:32.045 --> 00:07:33.575
Said Mrs Warren.

00:07:33.635 --> 00:07:36.486
Look, here are the notes he writes.

00:07:36.546 --> 00:07:39.117
This one says, soap.

00:07:39.177 --> 00:07:42.069
Here's another one that says, match.

00:07:42.129 --> 00:07:44.410
And this is one he wrote this morning.

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:47.002
It says, daily gazette.

00:07:47.062 --> 00:07:51.006
I leave the newspaper with his breakfast every morning.

00:07:51.066 --> 00:07:57.391
Mrs. Warren gave Holmes the two pieces of paper, and he looked at them with interest.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:59.402
Oh, dear Watson.

00:07:59.462 --> 00:08:00.573
Said Holmes.

00:08:00.633 --> 00:08:03.496
This is really a bit strange.

00:08:03.556 --> 00:08:07.304
Why does this man write in capital letters?

00:08:07.364 --> 00:08:10.980
It is much easier and quicker to write normally.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:12.982
What do you think, Watson?

00:08:13.042 --> 00:08:18.124
He probably doesn't want Mrs. Warren to see his handwriting, I said.

00:08:18.184 --> 00:08:19.455
But why?

00:08:19.515 --> 00:08:20.926
Asked Holmes.

00:08:20.986 --> 00:08:22.856
Perhaps you're right, Watson.

00:08:22.916 --> 00:08:25.418
But why are his messages so short?

00:08:25.478 --> 00:08:28.089
Why doesn't he write in sentences?

00:08:28.149 --> 00:08:33.636
I really don't know, I said.

00:08:33.696 --> 00:08:44.753
Chapter 2 Secret Messages Holmes looked at the two pieces of paper carefully.

00:08:44.813 --> 00:08:47.074
This is interesting.

00:08:47.134 --> 00:08:48.204
He said.

00:08:48.264 --> 00:08:52.525
He writes the words with a violet-colored pencil.

00:08:52.585 --> 00:08:58.767
Mrs. Warren, you said that the man is not tall or short and has dark hair and a beard.

00:08:58.827 --> 00:09:00.284
How on is he?

00:09:00.344 --> 00:09:02.003
He's young, sir.

00:09:02.063 --> 00:09:03.414
Not over thirty.

00:09:03.474 --> 00:09:04.996
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:09:05.055 --> 00:09:07.347
Can you tell me something more about him?

00:09:07.407 --> 00:09:08.618
Asked Holmes.

00:09:08.678 --> 00:09:12.691
He spoke good English, but I'm sure he's a foreigner.

00:09:12.751 --> 00:09:14.202
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:09:14.262 --> 00:09:15.784
Was he well-dressed?

00:09:15.844 --> 00:09:16.894
Asked Holmes.

00:09:16.954 --> 00:09:20.777
Yes, he was very well-dressed in dark clothes.

00:09:20.838 --> 00:09:22.488
Answered Mrs. Warren.

00:09:22.548 --> 00:09:24.320
Did he tell you his name?

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:25.591
Asked Holmes.

00:09:25.651 --> 00:09:26.667
No, sir.

00:09:26.727 --> 00:09:28.312
answered Mrs. Warren.

00:09:28.372 --> 00:09:31.434
Does he receive any letters or friends?

00:09:31.494 --> 00:09:32.654
asked Holmes.

00:09:32.714 --> 00:09:33.855
No, sir.

00:09:33.915 --> 00:09:35.696
answered Mrs. Warren.

00:09:35.756 --> 00:09:39.667
Do you or the girl go into his room to clean it?

00:09:39.727 --> 00:09:41.018
asked Holmes.

00:09:41.078 --> 00:09:42.028
No, sir.

00:09:42.088 --> 00:09:43.119
He does it.

00:09:43.179 --> 00:09:44.649
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:09:44.709 --> 00:09:45.750
Goodness.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:46.900
Said Holmes.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.951
That is surprising.

00:09:49.011 --> 00:09:51.082
Did he have any luggage?

00:09:51.142 --> 00:09:54.784
He only had one big brown bag with him.

00:09:54.844 --> 00:09:56.544
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:09:56.603 --> 00:09:59.224
We have to know more about him."

00:09:59.284 --> 00:10:00.526
said Holmes.

00:10:00.586 --> 00:10:03.418
Did anything come out of that room?

00:10:03.478 --> 00:10:09.212
Mrs. Warren took an envelope out of her bag and put it on the table.

00:10:09.272 --> 00:10:15.278
Inside the envelope there were two burnt matches and a cigarette stub.

00:10:15.338 --> 00:10:17.780
They were on his tray this morning.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:18.770
She said.

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:23.084
I know that small things are important to you, Mr. Holmes.

00:10:23.144 --> 00:10:26.495
Holmes picked them up and examined them.

00:10:26.555 --> 00:10:29.414
The matches aren't important, I think.

00:10:29.474 --> 00:10:32.185
He used them to light cigarettes.

00:10:32.245 --> 00:10:36.386
But this cigarette stub is very strange.

00:10:36.446 --> 00:10:38.467
You said the man has a beard.

00:10:38.527 --> 00:10:39.727
Yes, he does.

00:10:39.787 --> 00:10:41.408
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:10:41.468 --> 00:10:43.519
I don't understand.

00:10:43.579 --> 00:10:44.999
Said Holmes.

00:10:45.059 --> 00:10:49.230
How can a man with a beard smoke a cigarette in that way?

00:10:49.291 --> 00:10:50.981
He will burn his beard.

00:10:51.041 --> 00:10:53.676
The cigarette stub is too short.

00:10:53.736 --> 00:10:57.190
Perhaps there are two people in the room, Mrs. Warren.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:01.821
Oh no, he eats very little, not even enough for one person.

00:11:01.881 --> 00:11:03.612
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:11:03.672 --> 00:11:08.222
At this point I think we must wait for a few more facts.

00:11:08.282 --> 00:11:09.733
Said Holmes.

00:11:09.793 --> 00:11:14.604
After all, he paid the rent and he does not cause trouble.

00:11:14.664 --> 00:11:18.084
If he wants to hide, it is not your business, Mrs. Warren.

00:11:18.144 --> 00:11:22.286
We can't disturb him until there is a good reason.

00:11:22.346 --> 00:11:25.058
Now, I have a good picture of your problem.

00:11:25.118 --> 00:11:28.300
Please tell me if anything new happens.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:31.662
Thank you, Mr. Holmes and Dr. Watson.

00:11:31.722 --> 00:11:34.954
Said Mrs. Warren leaving the room.

00:11:35.014 --> 00:11:40.006
Well, Watson, there are certainly some interesting things in this case.

00:11:40.066 --> 00:11:41.446
Said Holmes.

00:11:41.506 --> 00:11:47.454
I think the person in the rooms is probably not the one who rented them.

00:11:47.514 --> 00:11:50.419
Why, I asked surprised.

00:11:50.479 --> 00:11:54.710
Well, first of all, there is the cigarette stub, said Holmes.

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:59.642
And then the lodger went out only once after he rented the rooms.

00:11:59.702 --> 00:12:05.704
He came back, or someone came back, when everyone in the house was sleeping.

00:12:05.764 --> 00:12:10.606
Was the person who went out, the same person who came back late at night?

00:12:10.666 --> 00:12:12.240
We don't know.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:20.196
The man who rented the rooms spoke English well, but the other person wrote, match and not matches.

00:12:20.256 --> 00:12:26.308
This person probably does not know English well, and that is why he writes short notes.

00:12:26.368 --> 00:12:33.031
Yes, Watson, I think the person in the rooms is not the same person who rented them.

00:12:33.091 --> 00:12:36.224
How strange, I said!

00:12:36.284 --> 00:12:41.718
Well, there is one thing we can do to find out more, said Holmes.

00:12:41.778 --> 00:12:47.680
The person in Mrs. Warren's rooms is alone, and does not receive any letters or friends.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:51.572
How can he receive any news or messages from outside?

00:12:51.632 --> 00:12:54.804
Only by an advertisement in a newspaper.

00:12:54.864 --> 00:12:56.574
There is no other way.

00:12:56.634 --> 00:12:59.315
And we even know the newspaper.

00:12:59.375 --> 00:13:01.360
The Daily Gazette.

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:08.397
Holmes opened a big book where he put the personal advertisement sections of the London newspapers.

00:13:08.458 --> 00:13:14.652
Here are the daily gazette personal advertisements of the last two weeks.

00:13:14.712 --> 00:13:19.676
Lady with blue hat at Piccadilly Circus.

00:13:19.736 --> 00:13:22.948
Jimmy, don't break your mother's heart.

00:13:23.008 --> 00:13:26.201
These don't interest me.

00:13:26.261 --> 00:13:28.158
Listen to this.

00:13:28.218 --> 00:13:31.674
I will find a way to send you messages.

00:13:31.734 --> 00:13:35.216
For now, this column, G.

00:13:35.276 --> 00:13:37.716
Look at the date of the newspaper.

00:13:37.776 --> 00:13:42.268
It's two days after Mrs. Warren's lodger rented the rooms.

00:13:42.328 --> 00:13:47.570
This mysterious person understands English, even if he cannot write it.

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:50.911
Perhaps we can find more, said Holmes.

00:13:50.971 --> 00:13:54.452
Yes, look, three days later.

00:13:54.512 --> 00:13:58.936
Take care, the clouds will pass, G.

00:13:58.996 --> 00:14:03.129
There is nothing for another week, and then another message.

00:14:03.189 --> 00:14:09.841
If I send a message, remember the secret code, 1A, 2B, and so on.

00:14:09.901 --> 00:14:12.822
You will hear from me soon, G.

00:14:12.882 --> 00:14:17.003
That was yesterday's paper, and there is nothing in today's.

00:14:17.063 --> 00:14:19.294
It could be the lodger.

00:14:19.353 --> 00:14:21.204
Let's wait a bit, Watson.

00:14:21.264 --> 00:14:26.502
We'll know more soon.

00:14:26.562 --> 00:14:38.742
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle started writing his stories about Sherlock Holmes in the late 19th century, during Victorian times.

00:14:38.802 --> 00:14:44.334
A lot of Britain's greatest writers wrote at this time, and this inspired him.

00:14:44.394 --> 00:14:53.646
During the 19th century, educational possibilities were slowly getting better, and more and more people were learning to read and write.

00:14:53.706 --> 00:14:59.684
Without the radio, cinema, or television, reading was the favourite type of entertainment.

00:14:59.744 --> 00:15:06.134
People loved reading novels, newspapers, magazines, academic books, and even comics.

00:15:06.194 --> 00:15:13.588
In the 1850s, the government started building public libraries in towns and cities all over Great Britain.

00:15:13.648 --> 00:15:22.742
Books were too expensive to buy for most people, but in these new libraries, more people had the possibility to read a lot of different books.

00:15:22.802 --> 00:15:29.281
The Daily Telegraph, started in 1855, became the first penny paper.

00:15:29.341 --> 00:15:34.600
The paper was a big success and quickly became the best-selling newspaper.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:39.734
Because of this, more and more people could read the newspaper every day.

00:15:39.794 --> 00:15:49.762
The first children's comics with funny cartoons and adventures also came out in the 1850s and children had great fun reading them.

00:15:49.822 --> 00:15:56.418
In Victorian times, novels were sometimes published in chapters in magazines and newspapers.

00:15:56.478 --> 00:16:01.382
Every week or month, people could read a new chapter of a story.

00:16:01.442 --> 00:16:07.660
This continued for months and sometimes years until the story was finished.

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:12.815
As a result of all these changes, some authors became very popular.

00:16:12.875 --> 00:16:14.777
Here are some of them.

00:16:14.837 --> 00:16:23.540
Charles Dickens 1812-1870 As a child, Charles Dickens was poor.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:27.974
He left school at a young age to work in a factory for long hours.

00:16:28.034 --> 00:16:31.127
He never forgot this bad experience.

00:16:31.186 --> 00:16:38.042
His first novel was the Pickwick Papers, 1837, and it was a success.

00:16:38.103 --> 00:16:47.136
He wrote 14 important novels, including Oliver Twist, 1839, and David Copperfield, 1849 to 1850.

00:16:47.196 --> 00:16:52.836
He wrote about the poor people and the social problems at that time.

00:16:52.896 --> 00:17:02.472
Anna Sewell 1820-1878 In 1877, Anna Sewell wrote Black Beauty.

00:17:02.532 --> 00:17:06.094
It was about the life and adventures of a horse.

00:17:06.155 --> 00:17:13.930
After reading this book, people began to understand that animals were living creatures and it was wrong to mistreat them.

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:28.425
Wilkie Collins 1824-1889 In 1868, Wilkie Collins published The Moonstone with one of the first detectives in English fiction, Sergeant Cuff.

00:17:28.486 --> 00:17:34.128
The novel became very popular and people started reading detective fiction.

00:17:34.188 --> 00:17:48.394
Lewis Carroll 1832-1898 Lewis Carroll wrote the first fantasy story for children called Alice's Adventures in Wonderland, 1865.

00:17:48.454 --> 00:17:50.596
It was a best-seller.

00:17:50.656 --> 00:17:58.650
Victorian children loved the book with its exciting adventures and beautiful illustrations by Sir John Tenniel.

00:17:58.711 --> 00:18:03.232
Robert Louis Stevenson, 1850 to 1894.

00:18:03.292 --> 00:18:11.705
In 1883, Robert Louis Stevenson wrote a great adventure book for young people called Treasure Island.

00:18:11.765 --> 00:18:15.616
It was about a young boy's adventures with pirates at sea.

00:18:15.676 --> 00:18:22.277
He also wrote other adventure stories like Kidnapped, 1886 and the Black Arrow 1888.

00:18:22.297 --> 00:18:26.426
Rudyard Kipling, 1865 to 1936.

00:18:26.486 --> 00:18:40.028
Rudyard Kipling wrote the Jungle Book, 1894, the second Jungle Book, 1895, and just so stories, 1902.

00:18:40.088 --> 00:18:42.449
They were very popular with children.

00:18:42.509 --> 00:18:47.931
Most of the characters of these books were animals.

00:18:47.991 --> 00:19:01.410
Chapter 3 The Face in the Mirror The next morning, Holmes was sitting at his desk with a big smile on his face.

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:03.501
Listen to this, Watson.

00:19:03.561 --> 00:19:06.772
He said, reading the newspaper aloud.

00:19:06.832 --> 00:19:16.658
Tall, red house with white windows, third floor, second window on left, after dark, G.

00:19:16.718 --> 00:19:19.399
This is a clear message.

00:19:19.459 --> 00:19:23.261
After breakfast, we'll go and visit Mrs. Warren's house.

00:19:23.321 --> 00:19:26.056
Good idea, I said.

00:19:26.116 --> 00:19:31.423
We got to Mrs. Warren's house and we waited for her in the sitting room.

00:19:31.483 --> 00:19:39.756
She suddenly ran into the room and said angrily, I must call the police, Mr. Holmes, but I wanted to talk to you first.

00:19:39.816 --> 00:19:42.107
That man must leave this house.

00:19:42.167 --> 00:19:45.528
Something terrible happened to my husband this morning.

00:19:45.588 --> 00:19:47.690
What happens to Mr. Warren?

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:48.860
Asked Holmes.

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:52.121
This morning he went to work before seven o'clock.

00:19:52.181 --> 00:19:57.284
He walked down the road a few steps, and suddenly two men came up behind him.

00:19:57.344 --> 00:20:01.156
They threw a coat over his head and pushed him into a cab.

00:20:01.216 --> 00:20:06.400
They drove around for an hour, and then opened the door and pushed him out on Hampstead Heath.

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:12.213
When he got home, the poor man was very frightened, and now he's sleeping upstairs.

00:20:12.273 --> 00:20:15.545
Very interesting, said Holmes.

00:20:15.605 --> 00:20:17.056
Did he see the man?

00:20:17.116 --> 00:20:18.696
Did he hear them speak?

00:20:18.756 --> 00:20:20.018
No, he didn't.

00:20:20.078 --> 00:20:21.409
Said Mrs. Warren.

00:20:21.469 --> 00:20:24.194
But he thinks there were two or three men.

00:20:24.254 --> 00:20:27.617
And do you think this has something to do with your lodger?"

00:20:27.677 --> 00:20:28.618
asked Holmes.

00:20:28.678 --> 00:20:29.678
Of course!

00:20:29.738 --> 00:20:31.098
said Mrs. Warren.

00:20:31.158 --> 00:20:33.760
My husband never had such a problem before.

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:35.950
I don't want that lodger in this house.

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:37.771
Money isn't everything.

00:20:37.831 --> 00:20:39.272
Wait a moment, please.

00:20:39.332 --> 00:20:40.652
Said Holmes.

00:20:40.712 --> 00:20:44.104
I think your lodger is in some kind of danger.

00:20:44.164 --> 00:20:48.755
Those men were probably waiting for him outside your door this morning.

00:20:48.815 --> 00:20:53.499
They thought your husband was the lodger because it was still dark at that time.

00:20:53.559 --> 00:20:58.052
When they discovered it was the wrong man, they let your husband go.

00:20:58.113 --> 00:20:59.923
What must I do?

00:20:59.983 --> 00:21:01.733
Asked Mrs. Warren.

00:21:01.794 --> 00:21:03.644
I want to see your lodger.

00:21:03.704 --> 00:21:04.994
Said Holmes.

00:21:05.055 --> 00:21:07.095
But how can you see him?

00:21:07.155 --> 00:21:08.556
Asked Mrs. Warren.

00:21:08.616 --> 00:21:10.566
No one can see him.

00:21:10.626 --> 00:21:14.938
He has to open the door and take the tray with his meal into his room.

00:21:14.998 --> 00:21:18.789
Watson and I can hide and watch him do it.

00:21:18.849 --> 00:21:24.634
Mrs. Warren thought for a moment and said, Yes, you can.

00:21:24.694 --> 00:21:29.358
There's a small cup at near his room, and I can put up a mirror.

00:21:29.418 --> 00:21:32.370
And if you are behind the door...

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:35.492
Excellent idea, said Holmes.

00:21:35.552 --> 00:21:37.674
When does he eat lunch?

00:21:37.734 --> 00:21:39.806
At about one o'clock.

00:21:39.866 --> 00:21:42.138
Good, said Holmes.

00:21:42.198 --> 00:21:46.412
Dr. Watson and I will come to your house before one o'clock.

00:21:46.472 --> 00:21:48.491
Goodbye, Mrs. Warren.

00:21:48.551 --> 00:21:53.350
At half past twelve, we were outside Mrs. Warren's house.

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:56.830
Holmes pointed to one of the house's opposite.

00:21:56.890 --> 00:21:59.261
Look, Watson, he said.

00:21:59.321 --> 00:22:02.971
Tall red house with white windows.

00:22:03.032 --> 00:22:05.882
That's the house in the newspaper message.

00:22:05.942 --> 00:22:09.693
Now we know the place and the secret code.

00:22:09.753 --> 00:22:11.433
Our job is easy.

00:22:11.493 --> 00:22:14.934
Look, there's a two rent sign in the window.

00:22:14.994 --> 00:22:17.328
It must be an empty flat.

00:22:17.388 --> 00:22:22.423
Yes, I'm sure it is, I said looking at the Red House.

00:22:22.483 --> 00:22:27.664
Holmes rang the bell of Mrs. Warren's house and she opened the door.

00:22:27.724 --> 00:22:31.516
I have everything ready for you, she said.

00:22:31.576 --> 00:22:34.736
Please come up and I'll show you the cupboard.

00:22:34.796 --> 00:22:36.477
Thank you, Mrs. Warren.

00:22:36.537 --> 00:22:38.097
Said Holmes.

00:22:38.157 --> 00:22:42.568
Holmes and I followed Mrs. Warren upstairs quietly.

00:22:42.628 --> 00:22:45.984
The cupboard was a very good place to hide.

00:22:46.044 --> 00:22:52.254
Mrs. Warren put the mirror in a position so that we could see the door of the lodger's rooms.

00:22:52.314 --> 00:22:55.386
We sat down and waited.

00:22:55.446 --> 00:23:01.098
Then we heard the lodger ring for lunch and Mrs. Warren came up with the tray.

00:23:01.158 --> 00:23:06.072
She put it down on a chair near the closed door and left.

00:23:06.132 --> 00:23:13.054
We heard her heavy footsteps going down the stairs and we kept our eyes on the mirror.

00:23:13.114 --> 00:23:17.064
When is the lodger going to open the door?"

00:23:17.124 --> 00:23:18.465
I whispered.

00:23:18.525 --> 00:23:21.016
Probably very soon.

00:23:21.076 --> 00:23:23.378
Holmes whispered back.

00:23:23.438 --> 00:23:28.039
Suddenly we heard the sound of a key opening the door.

00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:33.492
Two thin hands came out quickly and took the tray from the chair.

00:23:33.552 --> 00:23:37.524
But a moment later the hands put back the tray.

00:23:37.584 --> 00:23:43.520
I saw a beautiful but frightened face looking at the open door of the cupboard.

00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:48.366
Then the lodges door closed quickly, and the key turned.

00:23:48.426 --> 00:23:53.850
Everything was silent again, and we went back downstairs.

00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:59.433
I will come back again this evening, said Holmes to Mrs. Warren.

00:23:59.493 --> 00:24:06.116
I think we can discuss this better at Baker Street Watson.

00:24:06.176 --> 00:24:14.532
Chapter 4 Danger I was right, Watson.

00:24:14.592 --> 00:24:17.693
Said Holmes, sitting in his big arm chair.

00:24:17.753 --> 00:24:22.174
The lodger we just saw is not the man Mrs. Warren knows.

00:24:22.234 --> 00:24:25.035
It's a beautiful young woman.

00:24:25.095 --> 00:24:26.956
Yes, I said.

00:24:27.016 --> 00:24:29.006
And she saw us.

00:24:29.066 --> 00:24:32.637
Well, she saw something that frightened her.

00:24:32.697 --> 00:24:34.068
Said Holmes.

00:24:34.128 --> 00:24:37.108
Everything is a little clearer now, isn't it?

00:24:37.168 --> 00:24:42.330
A man and a woman come to London to get away from a dangerous situation.

00:24:42.390 --> 00:24:47.876
The man has some work that he must do, and leaves the woman in a safe place.

00:24:47.936 --> 00:24:53.721
This was not easy, but he had a clever plan, and he hid her well.

00:24:53.782 --> 00:24:57.436
Not even Mrs. Warren knows who the lodger is.

00:24:57.496 --> 00:25:02.290
She writes in capital letters because she has a woman's handwriting.

00:25:02.350 --> 00:25:05.379
She didn't want anyone to know about her.

00:25:05.439 --> 00:25:09.877
The man cannot come near the woman because their enemies could find her.

00:25:09.937 --> 00:25:12.908
So he writes messages in the newspapers.

00:25:12.968 --> 00:25:15.660
So far, everything is clear.

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:18.181
But what is behind this?

00:25:18.241 --> 00:25:19.222
I asked.

00:25:19.282 --> 00:25:20.842
Ah, yes, Watson.

00:25:20.903 --> 00:25:21.853
Said Holmes.

00:25:21.913 --> 00:25:23.854
What is behind this?

00:25:23.914 --> 00:25:27.566
Mrs. Warren's problem is bigger than I thought.

00:25:27.626 --> 00:25:34.038
We saw the young woman's frightened face, and we heard what happened to Mr. Warren.

00:25:34.098 --> 00:25:38.249
This is a matter of life and death, dear Watson.

00:25:38.309 --> 00:25:41.942
But the enemy does not know about the change of lodger.

00:25:42.002 --> 00:25:45.415
It is all very strange.

00:25:45.475 --> 00:25:51.330
It was a cold winter evening when we went back to Mrs. Warren's house.

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:56.724
We decided to sit in her dark sitting room in front of the big window.

00:25:56.784 --> 00:26:02.073
From there we could see a light on the third floor of the red building.

00:26:02.133 --> 00:26:08.930
Someone is in that room," said Holmes, moving closer to the window.

00:26:08.990 --> 00:26:11.452
I can see a dark figure.

00:26:11.512 --> 00:26:13.222
There he is again.

00:26:13.282 --> 00:26:17.844
He has a candle in his hand and he is looking out of the window.

00:26:17.904 --> 00:26:22.536
Now he is starting to send a message with the flame of the candle.

00:26:22.596 --> 00:26:26.258
Let's both try and read the message, Watson.

00:26:26.318 --> 00:26:27.868
One flash?

00:26:27.928 --> 00:26:29.374
That's an A.

00:26:29.434 --> 00:26:31.949
Now, how many flashes did he make?

00:26:32.009 --> 00:26:32.680
Twenty.

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:34.080
Is that right, Watson?

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:35.080
Yes.

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:37.941
He made twenty flashes, I said.

00:26:38.001 --> 00:26:40.381
That means T.

00:26:40.441 --> 00:26:41.442
A.

00:26:41.502 --> 00:26:42.452
T.

00:26:42.512 --> 00:26:43.272
At.

00:26:43.332 --> 00:26:44.542
That's clear.

00:26:44.602 --> 00:26:46.502
But now another T.

00:26:46.562 --> 00:26:49.153
That's the beginning of a new word.

00:26:49.213 --> 00:26:51.423
Hmm?

00:26:51.484 --> 00:26:54.159
Ten-ta.

00:26:54.219 --> 00:26:55.804
He stopped.

00:26:55.864 --> 00:26:57.945
That can't be the message.

00:26:58.005 --> 00:27:01.165
At tainter doesn't mean anything.

00:27:01.225 --> 00:27:03.976
Perhaps it's three words.

00:27:04.036 --> 00:27:06.356
At tainter.

00:27:06.416 --> 00:27:10.377
Or perhaps tainter is part of the person's name.

00:27:10.437 --> 00:27:12.668
There it goes again.

00:27:12.728 --> 00:27:16.729
What's that?

00:27:16.749 --> 00:27:18.889
A-t-t-e.

00:27:18.949 --> 00:27:21.309
It's the same message again.

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:24.054
How strange, Watson.

00:27:24.114 --> 00:27:27.400
Now he is sending a third message.

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:29.952
Atinta.

00:27:30.012 --> 00:27:32.444
He left the window.

00:27:32.504 --> 00:27:35.066
I think the message is finished.

00:27:35.126 --> 00:27:36.977
What do you think, Watson?

00:27:37.037 --> 00:27:40.640
I don't know Holmes, I said.

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:48.822
Suddenly Holmes laughed and said, Why, of course, the message is in Italian.

00:27:48.882 --> 00:27:53.904
The A at the end of the word means that the message is for a woman.

00:27:53.964 --> 00:27:59.188
It means be careful, be careful, be careful.

00:27:59.249 --> 00:28:01.060
How's that, Watson?

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:04.314
I think you're right, Holmes, I said.

00:28:04.374 --> 00:28:07.116
I'm certain, said Holmes.

00:28:07.176 --> 00:28:09.779
But be careful of what.

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:12.884
Wait, he's at the window again.

00:28:12.944 --> 00:28:17.869
We saw the dark figure of a man who started sending messages again.

00:28:17.929 --> 00:28:23.871
This time he sent the message very quickly, and it was difficult to follow it.

00:28:23.931 --> 00:28:26.212
Pericolor.

00:28:26.272 --> 00:28:27.782
What's that, Watson?

00:28:27.842 --> 00:28:29.874
Said Holmes.

00:28:29.934 --> 00:28:33.504
Isn't that danger an Italian?

00:28:33.564 --> 00:28:34.845
Yes, of course.

00:28:34.905 --> 00:28:36.675
It's a danger signal.

00:28:36.736 --> 00:28:39.696
Look, there he goes again.

00:28:39.756 --> 00:28:41.638
Peri...

00:28:41.698 --> 00:28:43.275
What happened?

00:28:43.335 --> 00:28:47.784
But the light went away and the third floor of the building was dark.

00:28:47.844 --> 00:28:50.375
The message suddenly stopped.

00:28:50.436 --> 00:28:52.136
What happened?

00:28:52.196 --> 00:28:55.128
Holmes and I had the same thought.

00:28:55.188 --> 00:28:57.910
Holmes jumped up from his chair.

00:28:57.970 --> 00:28:58.810
What's in?

00:28:58.870 --> 00:28:59.881
This is serious.

00:28:59.941 --> 00:29:00.841
He cried.

00:29:00.901 --> 00:29:03.082
Something terrible is happening.

00:29:03.142 --> 00:29:05.524
Why did the message stop?

00:29:05.584 --> 00:29:09.795
I want to call Scotland Yard, but we can't go away now.

00:29:09.855 --> 00:29:11.354
What shall we do?"

00:29:11.414 --> 00:29:12.314
I asked.

00:29:12.374 --> 00:29:15.835
We need more facts, said Holmes.

00:29:15.895 --> 00:29:17.086
Come on, Watson.

00:29:17.146 --> 00:29:19.586
Let's go and see what's happening.

00:29:19.646 --> 00:29:24.298
Holmes and I walked quickly out to the house and across the street.

00:29:24.358 --> 00:29:27.058
I looked back at Mrs. Warren's house.

00:29:27.118 --> 00:29:31.259
On the top floor I could see a woman's head at the window.

00:29:31.319 --> 00:29:34.570
She was waiting for the message to continue.

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:40.001
There was a man in a dark coat standing at the door of the Red Building.

00:29:40.061 --> 00:29:43.512
When he saw us, he called Holmes!

00:29:43.572 --> 00:29:45.003
Gregson!

00:29:45.063 --> 00:29:48.714
Said Holmes, looking at the Scotland Yard Detective.

00:29:48.774 --> 00:29:50.664
Why are you here?

00:29:50.724 --> 00:29:53.655
I'm here for the same reason you're here!

00:29:53.715 --> 00:29:55.016
Said Gregson.

00:29:55.076 --> 00:29:57.576
How did you find out about this?

00:29:57.636 --> 00:29:59.857
In a different way from you.

00:29:59.917 --> 00:30:01.358
Said Holmes.

00:30:01.418 --> 00:30:03.654
I was looking at the signals.

00:30:03.714 --> 00:30:05.261
What signals?"

00:30:05.322 --> 00:30:06.672
asked Gregson.

00:30:06.732 --> 00:30:10.014
The signals from the window said Holmes.

00:30:10.074 --> 00:30:17.406
They suddenly stomped and Watson and I came here to see why, but since the case is in your hands we can go.

00:30:17.466 --> 00:30:19.027
Wait a moment!

00:30:19.087 --> 00:30:20.428
cried Gregson.

00:30:20.488 --> 00:30:23.339
I always feel better with you with me.

00:30:23.399 --> 00:30:29.166
There is only one way out from these flats the front door, so we have him.

00:30:29.226 --> 00:30:30.412
Who is he?

00:30:30.472 --> 00:30:31.764
Asked Holmes.

00:30:31.824 --> 00:30:33.806
You don't know?

00:30:33.866 --> 00:30:36.088
Asked Gregson surprised.

00:30:36.148 --> 00:30:42.002
Well, for once I know more than you, Mr. Holmes.

00:30:42.062 --> 00:30:48.693
Chapter Five, Giuseppe Gorgiano.

00:30:48.754 --> 00:30:54.354
Holmes, Gregson and I were standing outside the tall red building.

00:30:54.414 --> 00:31:00.730
Suddenly, a tall man got out of a cab on the other side of the street and came to us.

00:31:00.790 --> 00:31:07.436
Mr. Gregson turned to him and said, Mr. Leverton, this is Sherlock Holmes.

00:31:07.496 --> 00:31:16.090
Then he turned to Holmes and said, This is Mr. Leverton of Pinkerton's Detective Agency in America.

00:31:16.150 --> 00:31:19.250
Sir, I am very pleased to meet you.

00:31:19.310 --> 00:31:20.370
Said Holmes.

00:31:20.430 --> 00:31:24.522
You're the hero of several important cases.

00:31:24.582 --> 00:31:28.955
The young American became embarrassed at the words of Holmes.

00:31:29.015 --> 00:31:32.347
I'm on the most important case of my life now.

00:31:32.407 --> 00:31:33.718
Said Leviton.

00:31:33.778 --> 00:31:36.539
If I can get Gorgiano.

00:31:36.599 --> 00:31:37.789
What?

00:31:37.850 --> 00:31:40.431
Gorgiano of the Red Circle.

00:31:40.491 --> 00:31:42.150
Asked Holmes.

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:45.412
Oh, is he known in Europe too?

00:31:45.472 --> 00:31:47.193
Said Leviton.

00:31:47.254 --> 00:31:50.618
Well, we know all about him, America.

00:31:50.678 --> 00:31:56.602
He's a terrible criminal who killed 50 people, and I want to arrest him.

00:31:56.663 --> 00:32:01.462
I followed him from New York, and I'm watching him carefully here in London.

00:32:01.522 --> 00:32:04.944
But Mr. Gregson and I followed him to this building.

00:32:05.004 --> 00:32:09.125
There is only one door here, so he can't get away.

00:32:09.185 --> 00:32:14.837
Three men came out of this building after he went in, but he wasn't one of them.

00:32:14.897 --> 00:32:17.928
Mr. Holmes talked about signals!

00:32:17.988 --> 00:32:19.548
said Gregson.

00:32:19.608 --> 00:32:22.600
I'm sure he knows more than we do.

00:32:22.660 --> 00:32:27.304
Holmes explained everything we knew to Gregson and Leviton.

00:32:27.364 --> 00:32:29.625
Gorgiano knows we're looking for him.

00:32:29.685 --> 00:32:31.736
Said the American detective.

00:32:31.796 --> 00:32:32.987
How do you know?

00:32:33.047 --> 00:32:34.167
Asked Holmes.

00:32:34.227 --> 00:32:36.648
Well, it seems that way, doesn't it?

00:32:36.708 --> 00:32:38.049
Said Leviton.

00:32:38.109 --> 00:32:42.741
This evening he sent messages to a member of the Red Circle.

00:32:42.801 --> 00:32:47.764
There are several members here in London, and they know we're looking for them.

00:32:47.824 --> 00:32:50.448
What can we do, Mr. Holmes?

00:32:50.508 --> 00:32:54.574
We must go up at once and see, said Holmes.

00:32:54.634 --> 00:32:58.038
But I can't arrest him, said Leviton.

00:32:58.098 --> 00:33:00.402
I don't have an arrest warrant.

00:33:00.462 --> 00:33:04.856
That man is in an empty flat that is not his home, said Gregson.

00:33:04.916 --> 00:33:06.908
That's a good reason to arrest him.

00:33:06.968 --> 00:33:11.065
Then perhaps the New York police can help us to keep him in prison.

00:33:11.125 --> 00:33:16.941
We went into the building and Leviton ran up the stairs first to arrest the killer.

00:33:17.001 --> 00:33:21.124
But Gregson wanted to be the first and pushed him back.

00:33:21.184 --> 00:33:26.778
After all, Gregson was part of the London police and we were in London.

00:33:26.838 --> 00:33:31.992
When we got to the third floor, the door of the flat was partly open.

00:33:32.052 --> 00:33:36.256
Gregson pushed it open and we all walked in.

00:33:36.316 --> 00:33:39.878
Everything was dark and silent inside.

00:33:39.938 --> 00:33:48.069
I lit the detective's lantern and suddenly we saw something terrible, a lot of blood on the floor.

00:33:48.129 --> 00:33:52.730
There were red steps on the floor that came from a closed room.

00:33:52.790 --> 00:33:57.551
Gregson opened the door and held the lantern in front of him.

00:33:57.611 --> 00:34:01.022
We all looked inside the room.

00:34:01.082 --> 00:34:08.792
On the floor of the empty room we saw a very big man with a circle of blood around his head.

00:34:08.852 --> 00:34:15.005
There was a big knife in his neck, and another bigger knife near his hand.

00:34:15.066 --> 00:34:17.127
It's Gorgiano.

00:34:17.187 --> 00:34:19.808
Cried the American detective loudly.

00:34:19.868 --> 00:34:21.580
Gorgiano.

00:34:21.640 --> 00:34:24.552
Someone got here before us.

00:34:24.613 --> 00:34:27.474
Here is the candle at the window, Mr. Holmes.

00:34:27.534 --> 00:34:28.655
Said Gregson.

00:34:28.715 --> 00:34:30.577
But what are you doing?

00:34:30.636 --> 00:34:35.580
Holmes lit the candle and moved it across the windows several times.

00:34:35.640 --> 00:34:39.575
Then he blew it out and put it down.

00:34:39.635 --> 00:34:45.290
I think this will help," he said, and walked over to the other two men.

00:34:45.350 --> 00:34:50.044
Mr. Leviton, you said three people came out of the building.

00:34:50.104 --> 00:34:51.376
Said Holmes.

00:34:51.436 --> 00:34:53.356
Did you look at them?

00:34:53.417 --> 00:34:55.218
Yes, I did.

00:34:55.278 --> 00:34:56.609
Said Leviton.

00:34:56.669 --> 00:35:02.754
Was there a man between twenty and thirty years old with dark hair and a beard?

00:35:02.814 --> 00:35:04.224
Asked Holmes.

00:35:04.284 --> 00:35:06.036
Yes, there was.

00:35:06.096 --> 00:35:07.580
Said Leviton.

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:10.946
I think he is your man," said Holmes.

00:35:11.006 --> 00:35:14.430
That is why I called his lady here.

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:26.318
We all turned around when we heard this, and at the door there was a tall, beautiful woman, Mrs. Warren's mysterious lodger.

00:35:26.379 --> 00:35:29.911
She slowly walked into the room.

00:35:29.972 --> 00:35:38.795
Her face was white, and her frightened eyes looked at the body of the huge man on the floor.

00:35:38.855 --> 00:35:43.334
Listening activity.

00:35:43.395 --> 00:35:45.685
Hello, Leverton.

00:35:45.745 --> 00:35:48.046
How was your trip from America?

00:35:48.106 --> 00:35:55.540
Well, I left New York before Christmas and spent Christmas Day with my parents in Boston.

00:35:55.600 --> 00:36:01.262
Then I celebrated New Year's Eve with my sister and brother, who lived in Salem.

00:36:01.322 --> 00:36:07.535
The next day, I left Boston Harbor on the new steamship, American Star.

00:36:07.595 --> 00:36:14.958
Last year I sailed from New York to Liverpool on the same ship, and it took me 25 long days.

00:36:15.018 --> 00:36:18.159
It was a bad trip with terrible weather.

00:36:18.219 --> 00:36:20.060
How long did it take you?

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:27.435
Well, it usually takes 15 days to cross the Atlantic, because this new steam share is fast.

00:36:27.495 --> 00:36:35.372
But this time we stopped in Newfoundland and Ireland to pick up some passengers, so it took us five extra days.

00:36:35.432 --> 00:36:37.783
Do you like travelling by sea?

00:36:37.843 --> 00:36:39.944
Oh, I love the sea.

00:36:40.004 --> 00:36:44.516
I was born in Boston, and that's right on the Atlantic Ocean.

00:36:44.576 --> 00:36:53.333
It's a great city with lots of things to do, but the weather is cold and wet most of the year, except for a short, hot summer.

00:36:53.393 --> 00:36:58.990
I like travelling by train, by horse, and by horse and carriage too.

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:02.752
I took the train across the American continent last year.

00:37:02.812 --> 00:37:05.925
I had to arrest a criminal in San Francisco.

00:37:05.985 --> 00:37:08.167
I love the sea too.

00:37:08.227 --> 00:37:12.981
My family has a house and a little fishing boat in my hometown in Scotland.

00:37:13.041 --> 00:37:17.624
If you're free this weekend, we can take the train and go fishing there.

00:37:17.685 --> 00:37:18.766
Thanks.

00:37:18.826 --> 00:37:20.606
I would love to go.

00:37:20.667 --> 00:37:24.640
Good, I'll pick you up early Saturday morning.

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:30.034
My hotel is near Piccadilly Circus, but we can meet at King's Cross station.

00:37:30.094 --> 00:37:36.800
Well, it's easier if I pick you up at your hotel at half past seven, and then we can go to the station together.

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:40.723
Sounds good, Gregson.

00:37:40.783 --> 00:37:47.042
Police and detectives in the 19th century.

00:37:47.102 --> 00:37:53.268
In the early part of the 19th century, there was a lot of crime in London.

00:37:53.328 --> 00:37:58.022
The city was crowded and criminals could hide easily.

00:37:58.082 --> 00:38:03.577
It was often not safe to walk down the dark streets.

00:38:03.637 --> 00:38:14.296
In 1829, Sir Robert Bobby Peel, 1788 to 1850, started the London Metropolitan Police at Scotland Yard.

00:38:14.357 --> 00:38:19.332
It was the world's first modern police force with around 1,000 men.

00:38:19.392 --> 00:38:27.178
The police wore uniforms and were known as peelers or bobbies, both because of Robert Peel's name.

00:38:27.238 --> 00:38:31.530
The work of the police was to stop crime in the city streets.

00:38:31.591 --> 00:38:35.853
At first the people of London did not like the police.

00:38:35.914 --> 00:38:42.567
But when there was less crime in the city, they started to understand that the police were important.

00:38:42.627 --> 00:38:48.367
After 1856 there were police forces all over Great Britain.

00:38:48.427 --> 00:38:57.508
The Metropolitan Police Force became a model for the New York City Police in 1850 and for other police forces around the world.

00:38:57.568 --> 00:39:03.719
In 1842 the first detective department was started at Scotland Yard.

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:07.740
It had only two inspectors and six sergeants.

00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:13.636
Detectives did not wear uniforms and they investigated more complicated crimes.

00:39:13.696 --> 00:39:21.613
In 1878, the Detective Department became the Criminal Investigation Department, CID.

00:39:21.673 --> 00:39:26.264
Mr. Gregson, in the Red Circle, worked for this department.

00:39:26.324 --> 00:39:33.716
In the Red Circle, Mr. Leviton worked for the Pinkerton Detective Agency in the United States.

00:39:33.776 --> 00:39:42.836
This agency was created by Alan Pinkerton in Chicago in 1850, and today it is the oldest in America.

00:39:42.896 --> 00:39:49.907
Alan Pinkerton became famous when he discovered a plan to kill President-elect Abraham Lincoln.

00:39:49.967 --> 00:39:58.682
During the American Civil War, President Lincoln used Pinkerton detectives to protect himself and his family.

00:39:58.742 --> 00:40:04.574
Sometimes detectives did not do a good job and made a lot of mistakes.

00:40:04.634 --> 00:40:08.246
Other times it took them months to solve a case.

00:40:08.306 --> 00:40:12.114
And at times they never solved the case.

00:40:12.174 --> 00:40:19.594
Some people paid a private detective, like the character of Sherlock Holmes, to solve difficult cases.

00:40:19.654 --> 00:40:28.700
In Conan Doyle's stories, Sherlock Holmes solved cases with modern scientific methods called forensic methods.

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:38.234
He used chemical analysis, microscopes, fingerprints, photographs, studies of handwriting, and footprints.

00:40:38.294 --> 00:40:42.711
In real life, detectives did not use these methods yet.

00:40:42.771 --> 00:40:57.228
In 1901, Scotland Yard started using fingerprints to solve cases.

00:40:57.288 --> 00:41:05.034
The beautiful young woman stood in the empty room and looked sadly at the body of the dead man.

00:41:05.094 --> 00:41:09.452
Then she looked at us with her dark eyes.

00:41:09.513 --> 00:41:11.898
But you, are you the police?

00:41:11.958 --> 00:41:12.970
She asked.

00:41:13.030 --> 00:41:14.981
Yes, we are the police.

00:41:15.041 --> 00:41:16.552
Said Gregson.

00:41:16.612 --> 00:41:19.665
She looked around the dark room.

00:41:19.725 --> 00:41:22.517
But then, where is Gennaro?

00:41:22.577 --> 00:41:23.778
She asked.

00:41:23.839 --> 00:41:26.100
Gennaro Luca is my husband.

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:30.174
I am Emilia Luca and we come from New York.

00:41:30.234 --> 00:41:31.975
Where is Gennaro?

00:41:32.035 --> 00:41:35.198
He just called me from this window and I ran here.

00:41:35.258 --> 00:41:38.132
I called you, said Holmes.

00:41:38.192 --> 00:41:39.254
You?

00:41:39.313 --> 00:41:41.074
How could you call me?"

00:41:41.134 --> 00:41:42.726
asked Emilia Luca.

00:41:42.786 --> 00:41:46.318
Your secret code was not difficult, madam.

00:41:46.378 --> 00:41:47.619
Said Holmes.

00:41:47.679 --> 00:41:53.022
We needed you here, so I flashed Vienni at the window.

00:41:53.082 --> 00:41:58.286
The beautiful Italian woman looked at Holmes with surprise.

00:41:58.346 --> 00:42:02.872
I do not understand how you know these things, she said.

00:42:02.932 --> 00:42:06.198
Giuseppe Gorgiano, how did he?

00:42:06.258 --> 00:42:11.741
She stopped, and then suddenly said, Now I understand.

00:42:11.802 --> 00:42:15.334
Gennaro killed this monster with his strong hands.

00:42:15.394 --> 00:42:20.648
Well, Mrs. Luca, said, Gregson, putting his hand on her arm.

00:42:20.708 --> 00:42:25.861
I don't really know who you are or what you are, but you know a lot about this case.

00:42:25.921 --> 00:42:28.870
Please come to Scotland Yard with me.

00:42:28.930 --> 00:42:30.880
One moment, Gregson?

00:42:30.940 --> 00:42:32.320
Said Holmes.

00:42:32.380 --> 00:42:37.334
I think this lady has several important things to tell us.

00:42:37.395 --> 00:42:40.887
Holmes turned to Mrs. Luca and said...

00:42:40.947 --> 00:42:45.710
Madam, your husband will be arrested for killing this man.

00:42:45.770 --> 00:42:48.412
What's Gennaro did is very wrong.

00:42:48.473 --> 00:42:50.614
Do you understand this?

00:42:50.674 --> 00:42:54.767
Now that Gorgiano is dead, we are afraid of nothing.

00:42:54.827 --> 00:42:56.418
Said Mrs. Luca.

00:42:56.478 --> 00:42:58.745
He was a terrible monster.

00:42:58.805 --> 00:43:01.282
But I know Jenaro did the wrong thing.

00:43:01.342 --> 00:43:03.874
Killing is always wrong.

00:43:03.934 --> 00:43:09.458
She was silent for a moment, and her dark eyes were sad.

00:43:09.518 --> 00:43:15.922
If I tell you the whole story, perhaps you will understand why my husband killed him.

00:43:15.982 --> 00:43:18.964
Very well, said Holmes.

00:43:19.024 --> 00:43:23.247
Let's close this door and leave everything here.

00:43:23.307 --> 00:43:27.824
We can go with this lady to her room and listen to her story.

00:43:27.884 --> 00:43:31.580
We all went back to Mrs. Warren's house.

00:43:31.640 --> 00:43:36.464
Half an hour later, we were sitting in Mrs. Luca's small sitting room.

00:43:36.524 --> 00:43:40.357
She started telling us her unusual story.

00:43:40.417 --> 00:43:45.280
She spoke very quickly, but her English was not good.

00:43:45.341 --> 00:43:49.184
I was born in Pazilipo, near the city of Naples, Italy.

00:43:49.244 --> 00:43:54.783
She said, I am the daughter of Augusto Barrelli, a very important man.

00:43:54.843 --> 00:43:58.429
Genaro worked for my father and I loved him.

00:43:58.490 --> 00:44:01.652
He was poor but he was handsome and kind.

00:44:01.712 --> 00:44:05.873
My father did not let me marry him, so we ran away together.

00:44:05.934 --> 00:44:08.254
We got married in another city.

00:44:08.314 --> 00:44:11.896
Then I sold my jewels to get the money to go to America.

00:44:11.956 --> 00:44:15.678
We went to New York City four years ago and stayed there.

00:44:15.739 --> 00:44:19.038
There were a lot of Italians in New York and we liked it.

00:44:19.098 --> 00:44:21.578
At first everything went well.

00:44:21.638 --> 00:44:27.982
Genaro helped an Italian gentleman called Tito Castellote and became his good friend.

00:44:28.042 --> 00:44:34.496
Mr. Castellote owns a big company in New York and more than 300 men work for him.

00:44:34.556 --> 00:44:39.990
He gave Genaro an important job in his company and was always kind to him.

00:44:40.050 --> 00:44:45.044
Mr. Castellote was not married and Genaro was like a son to him.

00:44:45.104 --> 00:44:47.620
We both loved him like a father.

00:44:47.680 --> 00:44:53.258
Genaro and I rented a small house in Brooklyn, and we were happy together.

00:44:53.318 --> 00:44:58.032
Then one evening, Genaro brought an Italian man home with him.

00:44:58.092 --> 00:45:02.924
His name was Giuseppe Gorgiano, and he was from Pazilipo too.

00:45:02.984 --> 00:45:05.376
He was so big, as you know.

00:45:05.437 --> 00:45:08.278
Everything about him was big and frightening.

00:45:08.339 --> 00:45:12.441
His voice was loud, and he moved his enormous arms about when he talked.

00:45:12.501 --> 00:45:15.353
He was too big for our little house.

00:45:15.414 --> 00:45:19.315
He never stopped talking with his loud, angry voice.

00:45:19.375 --> 00:45:22.035
He came to our house again and again.

00:45:22.095 --> 00:45:25.846
Genaro was unhappy when Gorgiano was at our house.

00:45:25.906 --> 00:45:29.047
But he sat and listened to Gorgiano talk.

00:45:29.107 --> 00:45:33.208
He was a very bad man, and he was always angry.

00:45:33.268 --> 00:45:38.189
We were all listening carefully to Mrs. Luca's long story.

00:45:38.249 --> 00:45:40.420
I knew something was wrong.

00:45:40.480 --> 00:45:43.675
My poor Genaro was terribly afraid of him.

00:45:43.735 --> 00:45:49.564
One night, Genaro told me his long story, and I became very sad.

00:45:49.624 --> 00:45:54.478
When Genaro was young, he was poor, and the world was against him.

00:45:54.538 --> 00:45:59.662
He became a member of a secret society in Naples called the Red Circle.

00:45:59.723 --> 00:46:04.506
It was part of the old Carbonari, and it did terrible things.

00:46:04.567 --> 00:46:09.342
The members of the secret society had to follow the rules and could not leave it.

00:46:09.402 --> 00:46:15.426
When we went to America, Janaro was happy because he was far from Naples and the Red Circle.

00:46:15.486 --> 00:46:17.947
He wanted to forget about it.

00:46:18.007 --> 00:46:22.548
But one evening, he met a member of the Red Circle in New York.

00:46:22.608 --> 00:46:24.988
It was Giuseppe Gorgiano.

00:46:25.048 --> 00:46:31.239
In the south of Italy, people called him Death, because he was a terrible killer.

00:46:31.299 --> 00:46:35.280
He went to New York because the Italian police wanted to arrest him.

00:46:35.340 --> 00:46:41.776
And in his new city, he started the Red Circle again.

00:46:41.837 --> 00:46:54.462
Chapter 7 The Red Circle Mrs. Luca continued her story and we all listened.

00:46:54.522 --> 00:47:00.478
One evening, Janaro showed me a letter from Gorgiano with a red circle at the top.

00:47:00.538 --> 00:47:05.592
He said there was an important meeting on a certain day and Janaro had to go.

00:47:05.652 --> 00:47:09.727
This was terrible, but then something worse happened.

00:47:09.787 --> 00:47:12.910
Gorgiano always came to see us in the evening.

00:47:12.970 --> 00:47:15.772
He talked to me a lot and I did not like it.

00:47:15.832 --> 00:47:19.384
He always looked at me with his big ugly eyes.

00:47:19.444 --> 00:47:21.466
I did not know what to do.

00:47:21.526 --> 00:47:25.788
Then one night, Gorgiano came to her house when I was alone.

00:47:25.848 --> 00:47:30.241
Genaro was still at work, and Gorgiano told me his secret.

00:47:30.301 --> 00:47:31.642
He loved me.

00:47:31.703 --> 00:47:34.764
He took me in his huge arms and kissed me.

00:47:34.824 --> 00:47:37.161
It was terrible and I screamed.

00:47:37.221 --> 00:47:40.148
At that moment, Genaro came home and tried to help me.

00:47:40.208 --> 00:47:43.970
He and Gorgiano fought a lot and Gorgiano knocked him down and ran away.

00:47:44.030 --> 00:47:46.382
He never came back to our house.

00:47:46.442 --> 00:47:49.794
Gorgiano became our dangerous enemy.

00:47:49.854 --> 00:47:55.498
Mrs. Luca stopped for a moment and looked at us with sad eyes.

00:47:55.558 --> 00:47:57.770
Then she continued.

00:47:57.830 --> 00:48:03.314
A few days later, there was the meeting of the Red Circle and Genaro had to go.

00:48:03.374 --> 00:48:06.374
When he came back, his face was white.

00:48:06.434 --> 00:48:12.283
to get money, the secret society blackmailed the rich Italians who lived in New York.

00:48:12.343 --> 00:48:18.424
The Red Circle did terrible things to the Italians who did not pay, it even killed them.

00:48:18.484 --> 00:48:24.766
The Red Circle sent a letter to a friend, Tito Castellate, and told him to pay.

00:48:24.826 --> 00:48:31.257
He was frightened and angry, and told the New York police about Gorgiano and the Red Circle.

00:48:31.317 --> 00:48:36.666
Gorgiano was very angry, and decided to blow up a Castellate's house.

00:48:36.726 --> 00:48:40.425
The members of the Red Circle chose Gennaro to do it.

00:48:40.485 --> 00:48:43.518
He had to kill his best friend.

00:48:43.578 --> 00:48:46.330
My poor Gennaro was terrified.

00:48:46.390 --> 00:48:49.342
He is a good man, he is not a killer.

00:48:49.402 --> 00:48:56.216
He looked at me and said, if I don't kill Castellote, Gorgiano will kill us.

00:48:56.276 --> 00:48:58.318
It was a terrible night.

00:48:58.378 --> 00:49:00.929
Gennaro and I were very, very afraid.

00:49:00.990 --> 00:49:04.516
We could not sleep and we did not know what to do.

00:49:04.576 --> 00:49:11.456
We talked all the night about Gorgiano, the Reda Circle, Castellote, and all of the problems we had.

00:49:11.516 --> 00:49:18.062
Finally, we decided it was too dangerous to stay in New York, and we had to leave immediately.

00:49:18.122 --> 00:49:19.984
But where could we go?

00:49:20.044 --> 00:49:25.328
I wanted to go back to Pozilipo, but Jenaro said that it was not possible.

00:49:25.388 --> 00:49:30.588
There were too many of Gorgiano's friends and other Reda Circle members there.

00:49:30.648 --> 00:49:36.026
So we decided to leave for London, because it is a big city and we know no one.

00:49:36.086 --> 00:49:40.097
We left early the next morning before the sun came up.

00:49:40.157 --> 00:49:48.090
We didn't bring anything with us, only clothes and documents, because we didn't want anyone to realize we were leaving.

00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:57.162
We went to the part, but before we sent messages to both Castellote and the police to tell them about Gordano's terrible plan.

00:49:57.222 --> 00:50:01.393
Well, gentlemen, you will already know the rest of the story.

00:50:01.453 --> 00:50:06.348
Guru Janu followed us to London, and Janaro found a place for me to hide.

00:50:06.408 --> 00:50:09.560
He wanted to go and talk to the Italian police.

00:50:09.620 --> 00:50:12.712
I never saw my husband during this time.

00:50:12.772 --> 00:50:15.946
I don't know where he lived or what he did.

00:50:16.006 --> 00:50:20.914
The only news from Janaro were the messages in the newspaper.

00:50:20.975 --> 00:50:25.141
Then one day from the window, I saw two Italians in the street.

00:50:25.201 --> 00:50:27.631
It was Gordano and another man.

00:50:27.691 --> 00:50:29.302
They were watching the house.

00:50:29.362 --> 00:50:31.512
I was alone and frightened.

00:50:31.572 --> 00:50:34.863
I read the Gennaro's messages in the newspaper.

00:50:34.923 --> 00:50:38.063
I waited at the window for his signals and saw them.

00:50:38.123 --> 00:50:42.394
I knew there was great danger because Gordano was near.

00:50:42.454 --> 00:50:46.732
Fortunately, Gennaro was ready for him when he came.

00:50:46.792 --> 00:50:50.440
Now, gentlemen, you know the whole story.

00:50:50.500 --> 00:50:53.452
A long and sad one.

00:50:53.512 --> 00:50:55.972
Well, Mr. Gregson.

00:50:56.033 --> 00:50:58.063
Said the American detective.

00:50:58.124 --> 00:51:00.604
Now we know the whole story.

00:51:00.664 --> 00:51:02.366
What do you think?

00:51:02.426 --> 00:51:05.036
Genaro Luca killed a man.

00:51:05.096 --> 00:51:08.098
Mrs. Luca has to come with me to Scotland Yard.

00:51:08.158 --> 00:51:09.478
Said Gregson.

00:51:09.538 --> 00:51:13.950
The chief of police has to hear her story and talk to her husband.

00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:16.453
Then he'll decide what to do.

00:51:16.513 --> 00:51:18.046
Well, what's...

00:51:18.106 --> 00:51:19.398
said Holmes.

00:51:19.458 --> 00:51:23.652
I think our work is done and it's time to go.

00:51:23.712 --> 00:51:27.806
He turned to Emilia and to the other men and said...

00:51:27.866 --> 00:51:30.058
Good evening, everyone.

00:51:30.118 --> 00:51:34.994
Holmes and I got up, put on our coats and left.

00:51:35.054 --> 00:51:39.588
It was a cold night as we walked out of Mrs. Warren's house.

